WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:07.683
Separated cells turn neural, yet their BMP receptors keep signaling.

2
00:00:08.703 --> 00:00:09.783
How can both be true?

3
00:00:10.783 --> 00:00:13.263
Start by putting the four results in one table.

4
00:00:14.217 --> 00:00:18.697
The intact cap: receptors working, Smad1 output high, epidermis.

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:23.627
The dominant negative cap: receptors jammed, output low, neural.

6
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.460
Separated cells: neural.

7
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:28.700
Leave their output for a moment.

8
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.040
Separated cells with BMP4: output high enough, epidermis.

9
00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:37.383
The output column is the model's reading.

10
00:00:38.263 --> 00:00:40.543
It was not measured directly in every row.

11
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:43.313
Read down the last two columns.

12
00:00:43.313 --> 00:00:47.733
In these four conditions, where the dial was high, the cells made skin.

13
00:00:48.253 --> 00:00:50.413
Where it was low, they made neural tissue.

14
00:00:51.483 --> 00:00:56.863
In a 1997 review, Hemmati-Brivanlou and Melton called this the default model.

15
00:00:57.833 --> 00:01:03.213
In these early cap cultures, neural is the fate frog ectoderm takes when BMP

16
00:01:03.213 --> 00:01:08.233
signaling is kept low, while the cells can still respond and their other inputs,

17
00:01:08.693 --> 00:01:10.553
such as FGF, are intact.

18
00:01:11.573 --> 00:01:13.273
BMP promotes epidermis.

19
00:01:14.217 --> 00:01:16.257
Default is an operational word.

20
00:01:17.137 --> 00:01:21.557
It names the outcome in these cultures, with competent cells and their other inputs

21
00:01:21.557 --> 00:01:24.297
intact, not a fate reached with no signals.

22
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.680
Now the third row.

23
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.320
The first explanation was dilution.

24
00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.910
Separation spreads the cells through a large volume of fluid.

25
00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.170
The BMP they pass to one another would be diluted away.

26
00:01:39.183 --> 00:01:40.443
It was a fair guess.

27
00:01:41.303 --> 00:01:45.023
If BMP spreads freely, the physics of diffusion supports it.

28
00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:47.713
But later work tested it.

29
00:01:48.373 --> 00:01:51.353
BMP receptors keep signaling in separated cells.

30
00:01:52.233 --> 00:01:53.453
So how could the dial fall?

31
00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:03.250
Separation switches on lasting signaling by MAPK.

32
00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:08.590
It is a kinase cascade, usually started by growth factors such as FGF.

33
00:02:12.183 --> 00:02:16.223
MAPK phosphorylates Smad1 in its middle, or linker, region.

34
00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:21.027
That can keep Smad1 out of the nucleus and mark it for destruction.

35
00:02:22.117 --> 00:02:24.597
The cells need this activity to turn neural.

36
00:02:25.457 --> 00:02:27.697
The dial still goes down, by a different hand.

37
00:02:28.683 --> 00:02:31.743
the fourth row? The damping is partial.

38
00:02:31.743 --> 00:02:36.043
So enough added BMP4 can push the output back up.

39
00:02:36.933 --> 00:02:40.493
That is why the rule names the dial, Smad1 in the nucleus.

40
00:02:41.413 --> 00:02:43.213
It does not name BMP in the fluid.

41
00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:47.047
The takeaway: three hands can turn the dial down.

42
00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:50.367
Outside the cell, at the membrane, or inside it.
